      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To characterize a guinea pig behavior model of bladder pain due to intravesical antigen infusion and to determine the role of neurokinin receptor subtypes in mediating this behavior."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "The influence of subtype-selective neurokinin receptor antagonists on increased abdominal licking behavior in response to intravesical antigen infusion in guinea pigs immunized with ovalbumin (OA) was determined."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Intravesical OA infusion for 30 minutes induced a significantly greater frequency (about 3-fold) of abdominal licking behavior than during either the 30 minutes pre-challenge or post challenge saline infusions. Treatment with IP capsaicin 7 to 10 days before OA challenge abolished the intravesical antigen-induced behavior. IP injection of the NK1 receptor antagonist CP 99994 (10 mg./kg. or 30 mg./kg.), 30 minutes pretreatment, inhibited the increase in the average number of abdominal licks during antigen infusion. The 30 mg./kg., but not the 10 mg./kg. dose increased the percent of animals showing antinociceptive activity (defined as 4 or less abdominal licks during the antigen infusion). The NK2 receptor antagonist SR 48968 reduced the antigen-induced abdominal licking behavior at IP doses of 3 and 10 mg./kg. but was ineffective at 1 mg./kg. The NK3 receptor antagonist SB 235375 (30 mg./kg., IP) did not reduce this behavior."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These results suggest a role for activation of NK1 and NK2, but not NK3 receptors, by tachykinins released from capsaicin-sensitive nerves, in the increased abdominal licking behavior response of guinea pigs to intravesical antigen infusion."
        }
      ]},
